.\" Title: vrunner-freeze
.\" Author: vrunner contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrunner-freeze.1

.TH VRUNNER\-FREEZE 1 "2025" "vrunner" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrunner\-freeze \- suspend a running command via SIGSTOP

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrunner
\fBfreeze\fR \fIPID\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
Freeze (suspend) a running command by sending it SIGSTOP. The command's OS process is paused but not terminated; all its state is preserved in memory. Use \fBvrunner thaw\fR to resume execution from where it was suspended.

This is the CLI equivalent of \fBPOST /api/commands/:id/freeze\fR. The subcommand first discovers a running vrunner instance (using \fB\-\-target PID\fR if specified, or auto-selecting if only one exists), then resolves the given OS PID to a command UUID via the instance's API, and sends the freeze request.

Freezing is useful for temporarily suspending resource-intensive tasks (compilations, data processing) without losing their progress. The frozen command remains visible in the web UI and CLI listings with its state unchanged. While frozen, the command does not consume CPU time but its memory remains allocated.

.SH ARGUMENTS

.TP
.B PID
OS process ID of the command to freeze. This is the child process PID (shown in \fBvrunner list\fR output), not the vrunner instance PID.

.SH EXAMPLES

.PP
Freeze a running command:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner list\fR
INSTANCE  PID: 12345  PORT: 9090  BIND: 127.0.0.1  DAEMON: no  DISPLAY: yes
  5678       cargo build                           CERT: -

\fB$ vrunner freeze 5678\fR
Command with PID 5678 frozen (SIGSTOP) on instance 12345
.fi
.RE

.PP
Resume a frozen command:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner thaw 5678\fR
Command with PID 5678 thawed (SIGCONT) on instance 12345
.fi
.RE

.PP
Equivalent curl:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Resolve PID to command ID first, then freeze:\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:9090/api/commands/abc123/freeze" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{}'
.fi
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Command frozen successfully.

.TP
.B 1
Error (no running instances, PID not found, connection refused, etc.).

.SH SEE ALSO

.BR vrunner (1),
.BR vrunner\-thaw (1),
.BR vrunner\-stop\-command (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR vrunnerctrl (1)
